---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";

const c = meta.counts;
const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "11 November 2025";
---

<Layout title="About">
  <div class="page about prose stack">
    <h1>About this tracker</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This site keeps a dated copy of every version of the Australian
      Government&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >AI transparency statements</a
      > and shows what changed between them. It is a <a
        href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au/cybernetic-studio/">Cybernetic Studio</a
      > project by <a href="https://benswift.me">Dr Ben Swift</a> at the <a
        href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au">ANU School of Cybernetics</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Every day a scraper visits each agency&rsquo;s statement and saves a copy if
      the text has changed. A language model (Claude Opus 5.5) then reads each
      change, writes a one-sentence summary, and records the statement&rsquo;s
      answers to a fixed set of questions. <a href={withBase("/reading")}
        >Reading the data</a
      > explains the results. The scraper, the prompts and every model reading are <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker">open source</a
      >, so any figure can be traced back to its source.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ben Swift started the archive in November 2025, the week the <a
        href="https://ministers.finance.gov.au/financeminister/speech/2025/11/12/launch-ai-plan-australian-public-service"
        >APS AI Plan</a
      > was launched, so the record opens on {corpusStart} and misses the
      statements&rsquo; first nine months (<a href={withBase("/reading#corpus")}
        >what that means</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2>Following changes</h2>
    <p>
      New and changed statements are announced on Bluesky by <a
        href="https://bsky.app/profile/apsaitracker.app">@apsaitracker.app</a
      >. The data is on the <a href={withBase("/data/")}>downloads</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>Who we track</h2>
    <p>
      Every non-corporate Commonwealth entity, from the Department of
      Finance&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.finance.gov.au/government/managing-commonwealth-resources/structure-australian-government-public-sector/pgpa-act-flipchart-and-list"
        >list of Commonwealth entities</a
      >, since these are the bodies the policy binds. Other bodies are added when
      we find a statement of theirs, often through the DTA&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/list-of-transparency-statements"
        >central register</a
      >, and a few that have never published are kept on the list. The <a
        href={withBase("/agencies")}>agencies page</a
      > shows who is required to publish.
    </p>

    <h2>Related work</h2>
    <p>
      Others have studied these statements as a single snapshot: <a
        href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.26075">Pan et al. (2026)</a
      >, who built the <em>AITS-101</em> dataset, and an <a
        href="https://www.admscentre.org.au/government-ai-transparency-statements-hard-to-find-new-report-finds/"
        >audit by the ADM+S Centre</a
      >. The DTA&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.dta.gov.au/articles/new-central-register-ai-transparency-statements-commonwealth-entities"
        >central register</a
      > lists the statements but keeps no version history. As far as we know, no
      one else tracks how they change.
    </p>
    <p>
      The approach follows archives of privacy policies such as the <a
        href="https://privacypolicies.cs.princeton.edu/">Princeton&ndash;Leuven
        corpus</a
      > and terms-of-service trackers. Similar efforts overseas record AI use
      itself, like the UK&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/algorithmic-transparency-recording-standard-hub"
        >Algorithmic Transparency Recording Standard</a
      > and the Amsterdam and Helsinki algorithm registers, or score transparency
      over time, like Stanford&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://news.stanford.edu/stories/2025/12/foundation-model-transparency-index-ai-companies-information"
        >Foundation Model Transparency Index</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Site redesigns, bot blocks and PDF extraction all introduce noise, and a
      statement filed somewhere unusual can be missed, so an agency listed as not
      having published may have. Treat the figures as indicative, and check the
      original via the source link on each statement page.
    </p>

    <h2>Citing the tracker</h2>
    <p>
      The tracker is archived on Zenodo. The DOI <a
        href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437"
        ><code>10.5281/zenodo.20842437</code></a
      > always resolves to the latest version. In scholarly work:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="about__cite">
      Swift, B. (2025). <cite>Australian Government AI Tracker</cite>
      [Software]. ANU School of Cybernetics. <a
        href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437"
        >https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20842437</a
      >
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      BibTeX and other formats are available from the Zenodo record or the <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/blob/main/CITATION.cff"
        ><code>CITATION.cff</code></a
      > file. Elsewhere, a credit with a link is enough:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="about__cite">
      Data from the <a href={withBase("/")}>Australian Government AI Tracker</a>,
      a project by Dr Ben Swift at the ANU School of Cybernetics.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The data changes daily, so give the date you read any figure you quote.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      The scraper and this site are MIT-licensed. The dataset, including the <a
        href={withBase("/data/")}>downloads</a
      >, is <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
      The statement text belongs to the agencies; most is CC BY 4.0, but check
      each agency&rsquo;s site.
    </p>

    <p class="muted about__build">
      {c.statements} statements, <a href={withBase("/reading#changes")}
        >{c.changes} changes of substance</a
      > across {c.revisions}{" "}
      captures, {c.profiled} profiled. Built {formatDate(meta.builtAt)} from commit{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker/commit/${meta.headSha}`}
        >{meta.headSha.slice(0, 7)}</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .about__cite {
    margin-block: var(--space-3);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-3);
    border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .about__cite cite {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .about__build {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
